1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,401
Between May and July 2026,

2
00:00:02,401 --> 00:00:07,087
Nvidia sold ninety-six billion dollars' worth of products.

3
00:00:07,087 --> 00:00:08,380
In three months.

4
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:12,050
And out of every dollar of those sales, about seventy-five

5
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:15,270
cents was left after paying for what it cost to make them.

6
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,607
That's the kind of margin you'd expect from software.

7
00:00:18,607 --> 00:00:20,840
Nvidia sells hardware, shipped in crates.

8
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:24,256
A margin like that usually draws competitors in.

9
00:00:24,256 --> 00:00:27,530
So here's our question: who can actually beat Nvidia?

10
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:31,438
The usual suspects are a rival chipmaker, China,

11
00:00:31,438 --> 00:00:35,615
or some clever lab that makes all those chips unnecessary.

12
00:00:35,615 --> 00:00:37,344
We'll look at all three.

13
00:00:37,344 --> 00:00:41,522
But the best clue is hiding in Nvidia's own annual report.

14
00:00:41,522 --> 00:00:46,420
In its list of competitors, it names Alphabet, Amazon and Microsoft.

15
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:49,790
Three companies that are also among its biggest buyers.

16
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:56,992
Nvidia started out making graphics cards for video games.

17
00:00:56,992 --> 00:00:59,583
Its chips, called GPUs, are good at doing

18
00:00:59,583 --> 00:01:02,680
thousands of small calculations at the same time.

19
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:07,230
That turned out to be exactly what training and running AI models needs.

20
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,408
But today, Nvidia doesn't just sell chips.

21
00:01:10,408 --> 00:01:14,566
It sells whole systems: racks of processors, the networking that

22
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:18,399
links them, and the software that makes them work together.

23
00:01:18,399 --> 00:01:21,972
In its own words, hundreds of thousands of its GPUs can

24
00:01:21,972 --> 00:01:25,480
be connected "to function as a single giant computer".

25
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:27,967
The software is the sticky part.

26
00:01:27,967 --> 00:01:31,022
It's called CUDA, and more than seven and a half

27
00:01:31,022 --> 00:01:34,267
million developers use it and Nvidia's other tools.

28
00:01:34,267 --> 00:01:36,940
Years of AI code have been written for it.

29
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:40,250
Moving away means rewriting and retesting that code.

30
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:45,706
The result: in its financial year that ended in January 2026, Nvidia brought

31
00:01:45,706 --> 00:01:50,515
in almost two hundred and sixteen billion dollars, up sixty-five percent.

32
00:01:50,515 --> 00:01:53,940
About ninety percent of that came from data centers.

33
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:57,300
Net profit: one hundred and twenty billion dollars.

34
00:01:57,750 --> 00:01:59,542
And it's still speeding up.

35
00:01:59,542 --> 00:02:04,719
That May-to-July quarter was more than double the same quarter a year earlier.

36
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:09,830
For the next one, Nvidia expects about one hundred and eight billion dollars.

37
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:13,080
So who, or what, could get in the way?

38
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:20,700
The obvious answer is AMD, Nvidia's oldest rival in graphics chips.

39
00:02:21,150 --> 00:02:22,509
AMD has real wins.

40
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:28,627
OpenAI agreed to deploy six gigawatts of AMD chips, and AMD gave OpenAI the right

41
00:02:28,627 --> 00:02:34,669
to buy up to one hundred and sixty million AMD shares as those deployments grow.

42
00:02:34,669 --> 00:02:37,766
Anthropic signed for up to two gigawatts.

43
00:02:37,766 --> 00:02:41,391
And according to Reuters, AMD said it could sell

44
00:02:41,391 --> 00:02:44,790
Meta up to sixty billion dollars of AI chips.

45
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:46,595
But look at the scale.

46
00:02:46,595 --> 00:02:50,476
Between April and June 2026, AMD's entire data center business,

47
00:02:50,476 --> 00:02:55,651
including its regular server processors, brought in six point seven billion dollars.

48
00:02:55,651 --> 00:02:59,409
Nvidia's data center business brought in eighty-nine billion,

49
00:02:59,409 --> 00:03:02,120
in a quarter that ends just one month later.

50
00:03:02,570 --> 00:03:06,738
AMD also keeps less of each sale: about fifty-six cents of

51
00:03:06,738 --> 00:03:10,690
gross profit per dollar, against Nvidia's seventy-five.

52
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:12,673
AMD is growing fast.

53
00:03:12,673 --> 00:03:15,740
Its data center sales doubled in a year.

54
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:21,567
But a rival about one-thirteenth your size isn't what keeps you up at night.

55
00:03:21,567 --> 00:03:22,180
Not yet.

56
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:25,058
Second suspect: China.

57
00:03:25,058 --> 00:03:31,040
Either Chinese chipmakers like Huawei, or governments shutting Nvidia out.

58
00:03:31,490 --> 00:03:34,289
The second part has already happened.

59
00:03:34,289 --> 00:03:38,070
In April 2025, Washington banned sales of the H20,

60
00:03:38,070 --> 00:03:41,625
a chip Nvidia had designed specially for China.

61
00:03:41,625 --> 00:03:44,953
Nvidia took a four and a half billion dollar

62
00:03:44,953 --> 00:03:48,130
charge on chips it suddenly couldn't sell.

63
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:52,069
Rules changed again later, but the outcome didn't.

64
00:03:52,069 --> 00:03:56,953
In October 2025, Nvidia's chief executive, Jensen Huang, summed it up:

65
00:03:56,953 --> 00:04:00,860
"We went from ninety-five percent market share to zero."

66
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:05,381
In December 2025, President Trump said Nvidia could sell a more

67
00:04:05,381 --> 00:04:08,741
powerful chip, the H200, to approved Chinese buyers,

68
00:04:08,741 --> 00:04:12,941
with twenty-five percent of the money going to the US government.

69
00:04:12,941 --> 00:04:16,559
But Nvidia says Beijing then restricted those purchases.

70
00:04:16,559 --> 00:04:19,467
Last quarter, those China shipments were less

71
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:22,310
than one percent of its data center revenue.

72
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:27,723
So Nvidia has been locked out of one of the biggest computer markets in the world.

73
00:04:27,723 --> 00:04:32,080
And in that same financial year, its revenue went up sixty-five percent.

74
00:04:32,530 --> 00:04:37,910
China has cost Nvidia a market, and over time it may produce serious rivals:

75
00:04:37,910 --> 00:04:42,157
Nvidia's annual report already lists Huawei as a competitor.

76
00:04:42,157 --> 00:04:46,050
But China isn't what's threatening Nvidia's lead today.

77
00:04:47,150 --> 00:04:49,200
Third suspect: an idea.

78
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:53,390
What if AI simply doesn't need that many chips?

79
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:57,031
On January twenty-seventh, 2025, a Chinese lab

80
00:04:57,031 --> 00:05:00,222
called DeepSeek made that idea feel very real.

81
00:05:00,222 --> 00:05:04,315
Its researchers said they had trained a strong AI model for

82
00:05:04,315 --> 00:05:08,270
under six million dollars, on less powerful Nvidia chips.

83
00:05:08,270 --> 00:05:12,155
That day, Nvidia's shares fell almost seventeen percent.

84
00:05:12,155 --> 00:05:17,635
The company lost five hundred and ninety-three billion dollars of market value,

85
00:05:17,635 --> 00:05:21,520
the biggest one-day loss for any company on Wall Street.

86
00:05:21,970 --> 00:05:24,163
The fear made sense on paper.

87
00:05:24,163 --> 00:05:26,810
Cheaper AI should need fewer chips.

88
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:32,272
But economists have a name for what often happens instead: the Jevons paradox.

89
00:05:32,272 --> 00:05:35,677
When something becomes cheaper to use, people find so

90
00:05:35,677 --> 00:05:39,340
many new uses for it that total demand goes up, not down.

91
00:05:39,790 --> 00:05:42,338
In the quarter of the DeepSeek scare,

92
00:05:42,338 --> 00:05:46,126
Nvidia's revenue was about thirty-nine billion dollars.

93
00:05:46,126 --> 00:05:48,950
Eighteen months later, it was ninety-six.

94
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:52,448
Cheaper AI didn't shrink the market for Nvidia's chips.

95
00:05:52,448 --> 00:05:54,000
It helped put AI everywhere.

96
00:05:54,450 --> 00:05:58,480
So: not AMD, not China, not efficiency.

97
00:05:58,480 --> 00:05:59,410
Then who?

98
00:06:00,510 --> 00:06:02,150
Follow the money in.

99
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:06,127
Nvidia now splits its data center sales in two.

100
00:06:06,127 --> 00:06:10,104
The first group it calls "hyperscale": the big public

101
00:06:10,104 --> 00:06:13,932
clouds and the largest consumer internet companies.

102
00:06:13,932 --> 00:06:17,984
Think of names like Microsoft, Amazon, Google or Meta.

103
00:06:17,984 --> 00:06:24,213
Between May and July 2026, that group alone paid Nvidia forty-nine billion dollars.

104
00:06:24,213 --> 00:06:28,040
That's more than half of all its data center sales.

105
00:06:28,490 --> 00:06:30,338
The filings tell the same story.

106
00:06:30,338 --> 00:06:33,573
In its last financial year, just two customers accounted

107
00:06:33,573 --> 00:06:36,404
for thirty-six percent of Nvidia's total revenue.

108
00:06:36,404 --> 00:06:39,638
Nvidia doesn't name them, and these direct customers can

109
00:06:39,638 --> 00:06:42,642
be companies that assemble servers for someone else.

110
00:06:42,642 --> 00:06:46,050
But the money ultimately comes from a short list of giants.

111
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:50,857
Those giants plan to spend more than seven hundred billion dollars this

112
00:06:50,857 --> 00:06:55,460
year on data centers and equipment, roughly double what they spent in 2025.

113
00:06:55,910 --> 00:06:57,656
Now put yourself in their place.

114
00:06:57,656 --> 00:07:01,202
With a seventy-five percent gross margin, only about a quarter of

115
00:07:01,202 --> 00:07:04,803
every dollar you pay Nvidia covers what the product costs to make.

116
00:07:04,803 --> 00:07:06,658
The rest is Nvidia's gross profit.

117
00:07:06,658 --> 00:07:10,040
If you can build a chip that's good enough for your own needs,

118
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:11,950
you keep much of that for yourself.

119
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:13,440
And they are building them.

120
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:20,076
Google designs its own AI chips, called TPUs, and now rents them to other companies.

121
00:07:20,076 --> 00:07:23,759
Anthropic signed up for up to one million of them,

122
00:07:23,759 --> 00:07:26,999
in a deal worth tens of billions of dollars.

123
00:07:26,999 --> 00:07:31,123
In February 2026, Meta reportedly signed a multi-billion

124
00:07:31,123 --> 00:07:34,290
dollar deal to rent Google's chips as well.

125
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:37,807
Amazon has its own chip, called Trainium.

126
00:07:37,807 --> 00:07:42,818
When Amazon agreed to invest up to fifty billion dollars in OpenAI,

127
00:07:42,818 --> 00:07:47,380
OpenAI also agreed to use two gigawatts of Trainium capacity.

128
00:07:47,830 --> 00:07:51,174
OpenAI is designing its own chips with Broadcom,

129
00:07:51,174 --> 00:07:54,796
aiming for ten gigawatts of them by the end of 2029.

130
00:07:54,796 --> 00:07:58,070
And Meta's own chip is about to ship in volume.

131
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:02,145
Broadcom, which helps build many of these custom chips,

132
00:08:02,145 --> 00:08:07,089
sold sixteen point seven billion dollars of AI chips in its latest quarter,

133
00:08:07,089 --> 00:08:10,253
more than three times as much as a year earlier.

134
00:08:10,253 --> 00:08:13,680
Almost three-quarters of that was custom processors.

135
00:08:14,130 --> 00:08:15,601
That's the real competition.

136
00:08:15,601 --> 00:08:17,439
Not a rival selling the same thing.

137
00:08:17,439 --> 00:08:20,170
Customers deciding they'd rather make it themselves.

138
00:08:20,620 --> 00:08:24,775
Jensen Huang's reply is that many of these custom chips are built "for

139
00:08:24,775 --> 00:08:28,752
one cloud or one service", while Nvidia's platform runs everywhere.

140
00:08:28,752 --> 00:08:32,075
And for now, the giants keep buying: their spending with

141
00:08:32,075 --> 00:08:35,340
Nvidia still grew thirteen percent in a single quarter.

142
00:08:35,790 --> 00:08:37,156
They're not leaving Nvidia.

143
00:08:37,156 --> 00:08:40,190
They're building a way out, and making sure Nvidia knows it.

144
00:08:41,290 --> 00:08:47,229
So if your biggest customers are also becoming your competitors, what do you do?

145
00:08:47,229 --> 00:08:48,566
You grow new ones.

146
00:08:48,566 --> 00:08:51,090
And Nvidia is paying to grow them.

147
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:55,256
The other half of its data center sales comes from what

148
00:08:55,256 --> 00:08:58,499
it calls "AI clouds, industrial and enterprise":

149
00:08:58,499 --> 00:09:02,823
specialized AI cloud companies, governments, large corporations.

150
00:09:02,823 --> 00:09:07,013
That half grew one hundred and thirty-eight percent in a year,

151
00:09:07,013 --> 00:09:09,580
to forty billion dollars last quarter.

152
00:09:10,030 --> 00:09:13,847
Many of these customers are young and hungry for cash.

153
00:09:13,847 --> 00:09:15,190
Nvidia supplies it.

154
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:19,491
In February 2026, Nvidia invested thirty billion

155
00:09:19,491 --> 00:09:22,781
dollars in OpenAI's latest funding round.

156
00:09:22,781 --> 00:09:26,230
Reuters reported that OpenAI planned to use

157
00:09:26,230 --> 00:09:29,600
much of its new money to buy Nvidia chips.

158
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:34,815
Nvidia has also committed up to ten billion dollars to Anthropic.

159
00:09:34,815 --> 00:09:39,067
In August, its finance chief said Nvidia had invested

160
00:09:39,067 --> 00:09:43,480
"nearly fifty billion dollars in the frontier AI labs".

161
00:09:43,930 --> 00:09:49,245
Add it all up, and at the end of July 2026, Nvidia held ninety-nine billion

162
00:09:49,245 --> 00:09:54,914
dollars of stakes in other companies, with another twenty-five billion promised:

163
00:09:54,914 --> 00:09:59,450
AI model makers, data center financiers and other private firms.

164
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,080
It goes further.

165
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:06,610
Nvidia sells equipment to some AI cloud companies, and also commits to rent

166
00:10:06,610 --> 00:10:08,896
computing power back from them:

167
00:10:08,896 --> 00:10:12,951
thirty-six billion dollars of these rental commitments.

168
00:10:12,951 --> 00:10:18,850
And in August 2026, it agreed to guarantee part of the lease and power payments,

169
00:10:18,850 --> 00:10:23,274
up to one hundred and five billion dollars, for a giant data

170
00:10:23,274 --> 00:10:28,140
center campus in Ohio, which will host Nvidia hardware for OpenAI.

171
00:10:28,590 --> 00:10:31,133
You can even see it in the profits.

172
00:10:31,133 --> 00:10:36,002
In the first half of this financial year, about twenty-four billion

173
00:10:36,002 --> 00:10:40,507
dollars of Nvidia's pre-tax profit, roughly one dollar in six,

174
00:10:40,507 --> 00:10:46,030
came from gains on its stakes in other companies, not from selling anything.

175
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:49,862
Critics call this circular: Nvidia's money goes

176
00:10:49,862 --> 00:10:52,885
out to customers and comes back as orders.

177
00:10:52,885 --> 00:10:56,196
Analysts raised that worry as soon as Nvidia's

178
00:10:56,196 --> 00:10:59,506
first plan with OpenAI was announced, in 2025.

179
00:10:59,506 --> 00:11:04,400
Nvidia calls these investments "a once-in-a-generation opportunity".

180
00:11:04,850 --> 00:11:06,185
And it isn't alone.

181
00:11:06,185 --> 00:11:08,784
AMD handed OpenAI those share rights.

182
00:11:08,784 --> 00:11:11,172
Amazon is investing in OpenAI too.

183
00:11:11,172 --> 00:11:16,370
Across this industry, sellers are increasingly financing their own buyers.

184
00:11:17,470 --> 00:11:19,630
So, who can actually beat Nvidia?

185
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:22,781
Not a rival chipmaker, at least not yet.

186
00:11:22,781 --> 00:11:26,361
Not China, which took away a market but not the lead.

187
00:11:26,361 --> 00:11:29,400
Not cheaper AI, which made the market bigger.

188
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:35,505
The companies best placed to beat Nvidia are the ones placing its biggest orders.

189
00:11:35,505 --> 00:11:41,090
They have the money, the engineers, and a reason: Nvidia's margin is their cost.

190
00:11:41,540 --> 00:11:45,646
Nvidia's answer is to make sure it never depends on them alone,

191
00:11:45,646 --> 00:11:49,231
by helping to fund a whole new generation of customers.

192
00:11:49,231 --> 00:11:52,554
That works as long as those customers keep growing.

193
00:11:52,554 --> 00:11:55,683
Which is why, with Nvidia, where the money comes

194
00:11:55,683 --> 00:11:58,420
from matters as much as how much there is.

195
00:11:58,870 --> 00:12:00,658
And the lesson goes beyond chips.

196
00:12:00,658 --> 00:12:03,366
A seventy-five percent margin isn't just a reward.

197
00:12:03,366 --> 00:12:06,670
It's an invitation to your own customers to stop needing you.
